POW.SYSTEMS CONSTRAINT / SEESAW PROBE =E2=80=94 21 Sep 2026 14:18 ICT

EXECUTIVE DELTA
The highest-information result is a stronger separation between true
constraints and fast-relaxing narratives.

1) BESS/grid connection allocation remains #1. Ofgem opened CMP470 on
17 Sep 2026. Around 90 GW of battery capacity is operational or in the
reformed connections queue versus ~29 GW projected requirement by
2035. NESO already removed 40% of grid-level battery capacity failing
2025 criteria, yet 83 GW remains prioritised plus 7 GW operational.
This creates a prospective shadow-price experiment: financial
commitment -> stay/exit/resize -> released MW -> changed connection
date/point -> energisation.

2) Generic UK EV-charger scarcity is now KILL nationally. DfT reports
121,171 public EV chargers at 1 Jul 2026, up from 119,080 at 1 Apr
2026. One CPO also removed ~1,270 chargers in June because private
chargers had been incorrectly exposed as public. That correction
demonstrates why POW should preserve charger identity/status
transitions rather than trust gross counts. Scarcity may still exist
locally or by power/reliability class, but must be proved
geographically.

3) Transformer/cable scarcity remains real but is visibly eliciting
supply response. Ofgem reports transformer prices ~75% above 2021,
cable prices ~100% above 2021, and HVDC cable lead times often >5
years. UK network-manufacturing turnover and employment have increased
>25% and >50% respectively since 2023. POW should therefore measure
relaxation velocity rather than merely label a shortage.

4) Heat-pump installer scarcity remains INFERRED, not confirmed.
Government still offers up to =C2=A3500 heat-pump training support and
explicitly routes consumers to MCS-certified installers. Training
availability is supply response, not evidence that transaction-capable
local capacity is binding. Require quote latency, installer
density/capacity and completed installations before promoting.

